  "requirement": "A `{req}` definition MUST use an opening fence of at least three colons immediately followed by `{req} <name>`. A closing fence MUST have at least as many colons as the opening fence. The requirement body MUST be all Markdown between those fence lines, including headings, examples, diagrams, and blank lines, and MUST NOT include text after the closing fence. Leading and trailing whitespace MUST be excluded when comparing the body with evidence.",
  "determination": false,
  "rationale": "**Verdict:** the span parsing is mostly sound, but the whitespace obligation is only half implemented and untested, so I judge the requirement not fully satisfied.\n\n**What is implemented well**\n- `definition_blocks` (src/lib.rs) finds colon fences through `colon_fence`.\n  - `colon_fence` accepts openers of three or more colons.\n  - The opener must be followed by `{req}`, whitespace and a valid name.\n  - The closing fence is the first line outside a Markdown code block that is colon-only with a colon count of at least the opener's.\n  - The body runs from `body_start` to `body_end`, which are the line boundaries around those two fences. This includes headings, setext headings, code blocks, blank lines and nested shorter colon directives.\n  - Text after the closing fence is excluded because `end` is the end of the closing line.\n  - A closing line with trailing text is not treated as a closer.\n- `collect_definitions` (src/evidence.rs, around lines 349–352) builds the declaration text with `without_options(...).trim()`. It reports a missing closing fence as an error.\n- Tests cover the main design space:\n  - `colon_requirement_span_includes_markdown_and_excludes_following_text` covers a body with a code block containing `:::`, an ATX heading, a setext heading, and text after the closer. It checks that a following requirement is parsed separately.\n  - `a_longer_colon_fence_contains_a_shorter_nested_directive` covers a four-colon opener with a nested three-colon directive.\n  - `reports_a_missing_closing_colon_fence` covers an unclosed block.\n  - `validates_full_names_requirement_text_and_input_lines` covers a body that contains a code block with a `{req}` reference.\n\n**Gaps**\n1. **Whitespace handling is one-sided and untested.** The requirement says \"Leading and trailing whitespace MUST be excluded when comparing the body with evidence.\"\n   - Only the declaration body is trimmed (`.trim()` in `collect_definitions`).\n   - In `validate_evidence_file` (src/evidence.rs, around lines 545–556), the evidence `requirement` string is compared raw with `text != expected.text`. Evidence that differs only in leading or trailing whitespace is reported as \"text changed\".\n   - No test exercises whitespace differences, such as a body with surrounding blank lines or evidence with trailing newlines.\n   - Under a strict reading, whitespace should be ignored on the comparison, not only trimmed from the stored body.\n2. **Untested edge cases.**\n   - No test covers a closing line with trailing text, such as `:::foo`. The code treats it as not a closer, which is a reasonable reading, but nothing verifies it.\n   - No test covers CRLF input.\n3. **Unspecified behaviour.** `colon_fence` accepts up to three leading spaces before an opener or closer. The span requirement does not mention this (\"immediately followed by\"). The code marks `fence_lengths` but not any definition that specifies indentation, and no test covers it.\n\nNone of these gaps carries a FIXME or other acknowledged marker in the source, so the determination is false rather than FIXME.",
